/* book Worked example 2: Q: A used-oil laboratory measures an engine oil at 100 °C: dynamic viscosity 12.0 mPa·s and density 0.84 g/cm³ (840 kg/m³). Find its kinematic viscosity in mm²/s and say which SAE hot grade band it falls in. The bands at 100 °C are: 30, 9.3 to below 12.5; 40, 12.5 to below 16.3; 50, 16.3 to below 21.9 mm²/s.
   1. Kinematic viscosity is dynamic viscosity divided by density: ν = μ ÷ ρ.
   2. In SI units: μ = 12.0 mPa·s = 0.0120 Pa·s; ρ = 840 kg/m³.
   3. ν = 0.0120 ÷ 840 = 1.43 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s.
   4. Since 1 mm²/s = 10⁻⁶ m²/s, ν = 14.3 mm²/s (14.3 cSt). A quick route: mPa·s divided by g/cm³ gives mm²/s directly, 12.0 ÷ 0.84 = 14.3.
   5. 14.3 lies between 12.5 and 16.3, so the oil is still in the 40 band. If fuel dilution thinned it below 12.5 mm²/s it would have fallen to a 30, a common reason to change oil early.
   Answer: ν = 14.3 mm²/s, inside the SAE 40 band at 100 °C. */
