/* book exam traps: A higher viscosity grade is not automatically better. Too thick an oil starves narrow passages at cold start and raises churning loss; match the grade to ambient temperature and engine design. | The W in 10W-40 means winter, not weight. 10W-40 is one oil that meets a cold 10W limit and a hot 40 band, not a mixture of grade 10 and grade 40. | Flash point is not fire point and neither is autoignition temperature. Fire point is higher, and a high flash point alone does not prove good lubrication. | TAN and TBN use the same unit, mg KOH/g, but move in opposite directions. Rising TAN and falling TBN both mean the oil is ageing. | Pour point is not the lowest safe operating temperature. Oil can still pour but be far too thick to pump or protect bearings. */
