/* book: lead: No piston ring seals perfectly. On every compression and power stroke a little gas slips past the rings into the crankcase; this gas is called blow-by. Left there, it pressurises the crankcase, pushes oil out of the seals, and fills the oil with water, acid and fuel. Crankcase ventilation is the system that sweeps blow-by out of the crankcase. Old engines let it out to the air; every modern petrol */
