/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Blow-by leaks through the ring end gaps and past the ring faces when cylinder pressure is high.
   - An unventilated crankcase suffers four ways.
   - Blow-by fraction = V̇bb ÷ V̇air × 100 percent
   - V̇bb is the blow-by flow measured at the breather, in L/min; V̇air is the air flow into the engine, in L/min.
   - Early engines used an open breather: a road draft tube ran from the crankcase to just below the car, and air rushing past at road speed sucked the gas out, with fresh air entering through a breather cap.
   - Positive crankcase ventilation closes the loop.
   - Some syllabi separate a semi-closed system, whose filler cap still breathes to the air, from a fully closed one.
   - The PCV valve is a tapered plunger in a small housing, held by a light spring.
   - The engine management counts on a known PCV flow at idle.
   - A turbocharged petrol engine has vacuum in the manifold only at part load; under boost the manifold is above atmospheric, and gas would be forced backwards into the crankcase.
*/
