/* book Worked example: Q: A 1.5 litre four-stroke petrol engine runs at 3000 rev/min with a volumetric efficiency of 0.85. A blow-by meter on the breather reads 25 L/min. Taking the blow-by density as 1.1 kg/m³ and its unburnt fuel content as 3 percent by mass, find (a) the air flow into the engine in L/min, (b) the blow-by as a percentage of that air, (c) the blow-by mass per hour, and (d) the fuel vapour an open breather would release in a two-hour drive at this condition.
   1. Intake strokes per minute for a four-stroke: N/2 = 3000/2 = 1500.
   2. (a) V̇air = Vd × (N/2) × ηv = 1.5 L × 1500 /min × 0.85 = 1912.5 L/min.
   3. (b) Blow-by fraction = 25 ÷ 1912.5 × 100 = 1.31 percent.
   4. (c) 25 L/min × 60 min/h = 1500 L/h = 1.5 m³/h. Mass = 1.5 m³/h × 1.1 kg/m³ = 1.65 kg/h.
   5. (d) Gas in two hours = 1.65 × 2 = 3.3 kg. Fuel vapour = 0.03 × 3.3 kg = 0.099 kg, about 99 g, released to the air by an open breather and burnt in the cylinders by a PCV system.
   Answer: (a) 1912.5 L/min; (b) 1.31 percent; (c) 1.65 kg/h; (d) about 99 g of fuel vapour in two hours. */
