/* book exam traps: Using gauge pressure in the pressure ratio. p₂ and p₁ must be absolute; a gauge reading of 0.6 bar is 1.6 bar absolute if ambient is 1.0 bar. | Forgetting charge heating. 1.6 bar is not 60 percent extra mass; with a 0.70 efficient blower the air leaves at 362 K and the density ratio is 1.33. A delivery temperature below the isentropic value (343 K here) is impossible. | Quoting gross power. The belt-driven blower takes crank power, so the exam answer is net power. | Assuming supercharging always lowers specific fuel consumption. It can, but at high boost the drive loss, fuel enrichment and knock control can worsen it. | Mixing knock effects. Boost raises knock tendency in SI engines, so compression ratio is lowered or fuel octane raised; it is not increased. */
